From: Ole Algoritme <olealgoritme@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:45:58 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] helios: Blackwell on NVK by PCI device ID

NVK reports no meshAndTaskShaderDerivatives, so an RTX 50 GPU on NVK
was reported as Ada (device ID >= 0x2600), and NGX picked its sm_89
kernels, which NVK cannot run on sm_120. On NVK an NVIDIA device ID of
0x2900 or more (GB100; GB20x from 0x2b00) is now reported as GB200.
DXVK_NVAPI_GPU_ARCH still overrides it.
---
 src/nvapi/nvapi_adapter.cpp | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/nvapi/nvapi_adapter.cpp b/src/nvapi/nvapi_adapter.cpp
index a1030d5..f18efab 100644
--- a/src/nvapi/nvapi_adapter.cpp
+++ b/src/nvapi/nvapi_adapter.cpp
@@ -280,6 +280,12 @@ namespace dxvk {
             && m_vkComputeShaderDerivativesProperties.meshAndTaskShaderDerivatives)
             return NV_GPU_ARCHITECTURE_GB200;
 
+        // NVK does not report meshAndTaskShaderDerivatives (it has no mesh
+        // shaders); Blackwell PCI device IDs start at 0x2900 (GB100, GB20x
+        // from 0x2b00), Ada ends below. NGX picks its sm_120 kernels by this.
+        if (HasNvkDriver() && m_vkProperties.vendorID == NvidiaPciVendorId && m_vkProperties.deviceID >= 0x2900)
+            return NV_GPU_ARCHITECTURE_GB200;
+
         // In lieu of a more idiomatic Vulkan-based solution, check the PCI
         // DeviceID to determine if an Ada card is present
         if (m_vkProperties.deviceID >= 0x2600)
